# Rust security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to Rust-specific,
OWASP-relevant risks and the safe standard-library/ecosystem API to use
instead. `unsafe` and panic-on-untrusted-input risks can appear anywhere a
crate touches attacker-influenced data, so this file stays broad rather
than narrowing to a single subdirectory.

## `unsafe` blocks

- Every `unsafe` block carries a `# Safety` doc comment (on the block or
  the function containing it) stating the exact invariants the caller or
  the block itself must uphold for the operation to be sound — not a
  generic "this is safe" note, but the specific precondition (pointer
  validity, alignment, lifetime, no concurrent mutation) that makes it so.
- Keep an `unsafe` block as small as possible — wrap only the operation
  that actually requires it (a raw pointer deref, an FFI call, a
  `mem::transmute`), not the surrounding safe code, so the audited surface
  stays minimal.
- Never widen an `unsafe` block or drop its `# Safety` comment to silence
  a compiler or clippy complaint; if the invariant cannot be stated, the
  code is not actually sound and needs a different approach.

## Panics on untrusted input

- Never call `.unwrap()`/`.expect()` on a `Result`/`Option` derived from
  network input, file contents, CLI arguments, or any other
  attacker-influenced source — a panic there is a denial-of-service vector
  (the process/thread aborts) even when the underlying bug is minor.
  Propagate with `?`, or handle explicitly.
- Guard array/slice indexing on untrusted-derived indices with `.get(i)`
  (returns `Option`) instead of `arr[i]` (panics out of bounds); guard
  integer arithmetic on untrusted values with `checked_add`/`checked_mul`/
  `saturating_*`/`wrapping_*` as the operation's semantics require, instead
  of a bare `+`/`*` that panics on overflow in debug builds and silently
  wraps in release builds — both are wrong for a value an attacker
  influences.

## FFI boundaries

- Validate every value crossing an `extern "C"` boundary before trusting
  it — a raw pointer from C is not guaranteed non-null, valid, or
  correctly aligned; check before dereferencing, and document the
  ownership/lifetime contract (who frees it, using what allocator) in the
  function's `# Safety` comment.
- Never let a Rust panic unwind across an `extern "C"` boundary — undefined
  behavior in most toolchains; catch it with `std::panic::catch_unwind` at
  the FFI edge and convert it to an error code instead.

## Dependency and supply-chain hygiene

- Run `cargo audit` (or the project's already-configured equivalent) when
  introducing or updating a dependency, or when asked to check for known
  vulnerabilities — it checks `Cargo.lock` against the RustSec advisory
  database.
- Pin dependency versions deliberately in `Cargo.toml`; avoid a bare `*`
  or an overly wide range that lets an unreviewed major/minor bump in.

## Deserialization

- When deserializing untrusted input with `serde`, apply size/depth limits
  appropriate to the format and deserializer (a bounded reader, a
  recursion-depth guard, or the deserializer's own limit configuration
  where it exposes one) — an unbounded deserialize of attacker-controlled
  JSON/bincode/etc. is a memory-exhaustion or stack-overflow
  (deeply-nested structures) vector.
- Never derive `Deserialize` for a type and then trust its field values
  as pre-validated — validate ranges/formats/invariants after
  deserializing, the same as any other externally-sourced value.

## Randomness and secrets

- Use a CSPRNG (the `rand` crate's default `ThreadRng`/`OsRng`, or the
  `getrandom`/`ring` crates) for anything security-sensitive — tokens,
  session IDs, keys, nonces; a non-cryptographic PRNG seeded predictably
  is unsuitable for secrets even when its API looks the same.
- Never hard-code a credential, API key, or signing secret in source; load
  it from environment/secret storage the project already uses.
